  drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/wifi7/hw.c: the WCN7860 entry (vdev_start_delay, rx_mac_buf_ring, reoq_lut_support off, the
    QCC2072 operations); wifi7/hal.c: WCN7860 takes QCC2072's HAL and registers and WCN7850's transmit-ring map and parameters
    (receive buffers return to software ring 1).
  mac.c, peer.c, dp.c: the order of a station's join on a chip with vdev_start_delay (the peer, its HTT peer map, the default
    routing value hash | ring << 1, one reorder queue per traffic identifier 0-16 with a window of 1, then the delayed start,
    the peer's association and the station up after the association); the station's parameters after a virtual device (the
    transmit encapsulation and receive decapsulation, the stream count, the three power-save parameters and their values, power
    save off); the peer's association fields of a legacy peer (the rate form 500 kb/s | bit 7 for CCK, the four-way-handshake
    flag clearing the authorisation); a station's start (passive with "no initiating radiation", the beacon interval and DTIM).
  dp_rx.c, wifi7/dp_rx.c, wifi7/hal_rx.c, wifi7/hal_desc.h, hal.h: the reorder queue descriptor (its 32 words, the header's owner,
    kind and debug pattern, the queue number, info0's valid / counter / access category / retry / window / PN / ignore-A-MPDU
    bits, the start sequence number, the three extension descriptors of a QoS identifier, the 128-byte alignment), the
    identifier-to-access-category rule, WMI_PEER_REORDER_QUEUE_SETUP's fields.
  wmi.h, wmi.c: the command and event identifiers (counted out of their enumerations) and tags of the station power-save
    parameter, the reorder queue set-up, the roam event; the device parameter numbers of the encapsulation, decapsulation and
    stream count; the peer parameter "default routing"; the frame modes (raw 0, native Wi-Fi 1, Ethernet 2); the management
    frame command's fields and its 64-byte inline copy; the roam event's reason values.
  dp_htt.c, dp_htt.h: the HTT peer map messages (types 3, 0x1e, 0x2b; unmap 4, 0x1f) and their fields.
The 802.11 frames of the join (WifiDot11.h: the authentication, the association request and its elements' order, the RSN element
and its suites, the answers, the status and reason codes) are written from IEEE Std 802.11 itself; no driver's code was read
for them.

